<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      THE LAUNCHER NAME IS A VOICE TARGET, AND THAT IS WHY IT LOOKS LIKE THIS.

      This is the name Google matches for "open X" and "play something on X".
      Three attempts, each of which failed differently, and the rule is the
      union of what they taught:

        "MusicD Remote Lite" came back as "Music d remote light". Three words
        to say, and a trailing capital letter no speech model has a place for.
        => ONE WORD, LETTERS ONLY, NO STRANDED CAPITAL.

        "Roon" comes back as "rune". A name absent from the speech model's
        lexicon is replaced by the nearest real word, and the request dies
        before anything is matched.
        => AN ORDINARY WORD, SPELLED THE WAY IT SOUNDS.

        "Remote" transcribes perfectly and still opened nothing, because
        "open remote" is how Google opens its own built-in TV remote. The
        phrase reached the matcher intact and lost to a feature that was
        already there.
        => NOT A WORD GOOGLE HAS ALREADY CLAIMED.

      "Jukebox" is the first name to clear all three: every speech model has
      it, it is one plain word, and Google has no feature by that name to
      intercept it.

      AppNameTest asserts all of this, including the reserved list, so the
      lessons cannot be quietly undone by someone typing a nicer product name
      in here.

      The project is still MusicD Remote Lite: the package, the repository and
      the name Roon shows in Settings -> Extensions are all unchanged. This is
      only what the launcher icon and the assistant call it.
    -->
    <string name="app_name">Jukebox</string>
    <string name="dial_name">Dial for Roon</string>

    <!--
      A voice verb's name IS the command: "Ok Google, open Roulette". It is a
      launcher label under exactly the same rule as app_name above, and
      VoiceVerbsTest checks it against VoiceName rather than trusting the eye.
    -->
    <string name="verb_roulette">Roulette</string>
    <string name="tile_random_album">Random album</string>

    <!-- The plain bar widget. -->
    <string name="widget_bar_description">Now playing, with transport. Tap the cover for a random album.</string>

    <!--
      The dial widget. These two names are fixed by the layout synced from
      Dial for Roon, so they are what they are; see tools/dial-upstream.json.
    -->
    <string name="widget_artwork">Album artwork</string>
    <string name="widget_description">The dial: album art in a volume ring, with transport and volume.</string>
</resources>
